WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.177 --> 00:00:05.889
I'm Shawn Hoffmeyer, director of the ambulance
service at Kestrel Falls Community

2
00:00:05.889 --> 00:00:06.489
Hospital.

3
00:00:05.889 --> 00:00:10.029
I came in from the base because Laura Hemmingsen
asked how my crews use the system

4
00:00:10.029 --> 00:00:11.574
and what we need from the new one.

5
00:00:11.574 --> 00:00:13.363
I've been director for ten years.

6
00:00:13.363 --> 00:00:18.028
We've got forty-eight paramedics and EMTs,
three bases, here in Kestrel Falls,

7
00:00:18.028 --> 00:00:22.357
Prairie Bend, and Cedar Hollow, and we run
about forty-two hundred calls a year.

8
00:00:22.357 --> 00:00:24.851
Our crews chart in the back
of a moving rig.

9
00:00:24.851 --> 00:00:26.822
The electronic patient care
report, the ePCR,

10
00:00:26.822 --> 00:00:30.818
has to work while we're bouncing down a
county road at night, with gloves on,

11
00:00:30.818 --> 00:00:33.854
and a patient who needs both hands.

12
00:00:33.854 --> 00:00:37.291
Right now the tablets send that report to the ED
when we arrive, most of the time.

13
00:00:37.291 --> 00:00:42.310
When it works, the ED nurse can see
our vitals, what we gave,

14
00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:42.910
and what we saw on scene.

15
00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:47.160
When it doesn't work, my medic is standing at
the nurses' station giving a verbal

16
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.876
report while the nurse is trying to
take care of somebody else.

17
00:00:50.876 --> 00:00:52.345
Things get missed.

18
00:00:52.345 --> 00:00:55.857
My biggest fear is that the new system gets
designed for people sitting at a desk

19
00:00:55.857 --> 00:00:59.041
in the hospital, and it fails our
crews in the field.

20
00:00:59.041 --> 00:01:01.435
We have dead zones on some of
the rural routes.

21
00:01:01.435 --> 00:01:02.977
No signal for miles.

22
00:01:02.977 --> 00:01:06.543
If the software needs a connection to save,
my paramedics lose their notes,

23
00:01:06.543 --> 00:01:09.185
or they stop charting until they get back,

24
00:01:09.185 --> 00:01:11.760
and they're trying to remember
details an hour later.

25
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.917
What time did we give the second dose?

26
00:01:13.917 --> 00:01:15.744
What was the blood pressure at the scene?

27
00:01:15.744 --> 00:01:17.393
Memory isn't good enough for that.

28
00:01:17.393 --> 00:01:19.733
What I need is tools that
work in the field.

29
00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:22.938
Charting that works offline and
syncs when we get signal.

30
00:01:22.938 --> 00:01:29.479
Tablets that work with gloves, and that you can
read in bright sun and in the dark.

31
00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:30.079
Big buttons.

32
00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:32.089
And a clean handoff to the
emergency department,

33
00:01:32.089 --> 00:01:35.851
so the ED nurse sees our report before
we've even unloaded the stretcher.

34
00:01:35.851 --> 00:01:40.243
For planning, put a working paramedic on
the design team, not just me.

35
00:01:40.243 --> 00:01:44.075
Somebody like Carlos Ruiz, who's on
the truck every shift.

36
00:01:44.075 --> 00:01:47.558
But understand that my people work
twenty-four-hour rotations.

37
00:01:47.558 --> 00:01:50.222
They can't come to a meeting on
a Tuesday at ten.

38
00:01:50.222 --> 00:01:54.097
Record the meetings, or let them join
from the base between calls.

39
00:01:54.097 --> 00:01:55.942
Training has the same problem.

40
00:01:55.942 --> 00:01:58.760
We can't pull a crew off the road
for a half-day class,

41
00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.428
because then there's no ambulance.

42
00:02:00.428 --> 00:02:04.319
Train at the bases, in short
sessions, on the actual tablets.

43
00:02:04.319 --> 00:02:07.442
And give them a practice version they
can use during downtime.

44
00:02:07.442 --> 00:02:11.435
What I don't want is ambulance as the
afterthought, the "phase three,

45
00:02:11.435 --> 00:02:12.935
someday" part of the plan.

46
00:02:12.935 --> 00:02:15.642
We bring in a lot of this
hospital's sickest patients.

47
00:02:15.642 --> 00:02:18.257
Heart attacks, strokes, car accidents.

48
00:02:18.257 --> 00:02:21.916
The first information the hospital gets
about those patients comes from us.

49
00:02:21.916 --> 00:02:25.436
If our part doesn't work on day one,
the ED is starting blind.

50
00:02:25.436 --> 00:02:28.137
If those needs are met, would I
support the change?

51
00:02:28.137 --> 00:02:28.737
Yes.

52
00:02:28.137 --> 00:02:33.803
Plexmoor's been okay for us, but it's old, and
the connection to the ED is shaky.

53
00:02:33.803 --> 00:02:36.633
If the new one works offline and hands off
cleanly, my crews will love it.

54
00:02:36.633 --> 00:02:40.527
But I need to see it work on a
rural route first.

55
00:02:40.527 --> 00:02:42.305
Here's what a good test looks like.

56
00:02:42.305 --> 00:02:45.076
Put a crew on our longest rural
route with the tablet,

57
00:02:45.076 --> 00:02:48.587
the route out past Cedar Hollow where we
lose signal for twenty minutes.

58
00:02:48.587 --> 00:02:50.440
Chart a fake patient the whole way,

59
00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.761
then see if everything shows up in the ED
when they get back in range.

60
00:02:53.761 --> 00:02:55.408
If it does, I'm sold.

61
00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:58.189
If it doesn't, we fix it before
go-live, not after.

62
00:02:58.189 --> 00:03:00.422
And one more thing my crews
would want you to know.

63
00:03:00.422 --> 00:03:01.978
They're proud of their charting.

64
00:03:01.978 --> 00:03:03.920
A good report helps the patient.

65
00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:05.577
They don't want to do it badly.

66
00:03:05.577 --> 00:03:08.520
Give them a tool that works
where they work.

